# Line endings, decided here rather than by whoever cloned.
#
# Without this, the working tree's line endings depend on the client's `core.autocrlf`, which
# Git for Windows turns on by default. This project claims Windows support and the suite is
# largely a comparison between text it generates — always LF — and text read back out of
# committed files, so a CRLF checkout puts the two on different sides of an equality. Checked
# by converting one page to CRLF: the metadata.json shape guard fails on it immediately.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises to LF in the repository and checks out LF everywhere, which is
# also what tsconfig's `"newLine": "lf"` already asks of the compiler's output.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so git neither normalises them nor tries to diff them as text. None are committed
# today — every recording is generated by `npm run fixtures` into a gitignored directory — but
# a checked-in sample would be silently corrupted by the rule above.
*.edf binary
*.bdf binary
*.woff2 binary
*.png binary
*.tgz binary
